WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.670
ED GLAESER: I'm here in the Harvard Art Museum in front of an exquisitely beautiful Virgin and Child,

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:09.050
painted by Sandro Botticelli, the legendary Florentine painter.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.900
Botticelli was part of a chain of artistic genius that flourished

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.470
in Renaissance Florence.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.313
It starts when Brunelleschi figures out the basic mathematics of linear

00:00:18.313 --> 00:00:22.870
perspective, how to make two-dimensional surfaces appear three-dimensional.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.330
He passes that wisdom along to his friend Donatello,

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.190
who puts it on the wall of Orsanmichele in low relief sculpture.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.030
Donatello passes along to Masaccio, who paints the Brancacci Chapel.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.499
Marvelous painting of Saint Peter finding a silver coin in the belly of a fish.

00:00:37.499 --> 00:00:42.164
According to Varsari, Fra Filippo Lippi, that less than saintly monk,

00:00:42.164 --> 00:00:45.535
learns his craft by sitting at the feet of Masaccio.

00:00:45.535 --> 00:00:49.150
Lippi was the teacher of Botticelli, and perhaps we see

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.620
the influence of Lippi's beautiful Madonnas in the Madonna behind us.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.620
This was a chain of artistic greatness brought together by the city of Florence.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.090
But this painting isn't just another beautiful Madonna and Child by Botticelli,

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.850
there's something special about it.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.860
Look at the buildings, look at their roofs.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.340
Look at the crow gables.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.540
These are northern roofs, the kind of roofs you would see in Copenhagen or

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.570
Amsterdam or Bruges.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:14.050
They're not roofs you would see in Florence or Naples or Rome.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:18.850
It's quite possible that this painting was crafted by the master of gothic buildings.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.960
A northern painter who came south to work with Botticelli.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.730
And in a sense, this painting symbolizes the deep

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.260
connections between the Northern and Southern Renaissance.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:33.970
The North, which gave us the detailed oil painting of van Eyck and van der Weyden.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.460
The South, which gave us linear perspective and

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.040
the glories of da Vinci and Raphael.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.060
These two Renaissances came together in part because these two

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.850
great urban civilizations, that of the Low Countries and

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.560
that of central Italy, were tied so closely by common interests and by trade.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.530
In the 15th century, the North and the South looked very similar.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.980
They were both free places of commerce and creativity.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.930
Places where artistic genius flourished.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.630
Two centuries later, the North was still at it.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.120
Amsterdam was the center of a great trading empire,

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.830
still churning out artistic masterpieces like Rembrandt's Night Watch,

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.530
while Florence had sunk into silence.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.210
One reason for this is political.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:17.758
Amsterdam had won its freedom in a hard fought 60 year revolt against their

00:02:17.758 --> 00:02:19.560
Hapsburg overlords.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.060
Florence had become a petty principality.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.280
The Medici's had moved from being merchant princes,

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.030
the first among equals who were eager to court popularity by sponsoring great

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:32.500
artists, to becoming petty despots resting on their laurels.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.800
And so the greatness of Florence faded, and yet

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.750
it is still here, that legacy of 15th century glory.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.990
Which reminds us of the miracles that happen when people come together and

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.120
are connected by cities.

